From 70a5d09421ed07cdedff035a53e3f7ac8929a886 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:34:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] doma: stop the screen going dead, and describe the features
 in README

def_screen_off_timeout goes from AOSP's 60 s to 30 min.

This panel has no backlight, so the dim step of the screen-off sequence
is applied as a near-black overlay -- DisplayPowerController takes
brightness from 0.397 to 0.05 -- and the display simply looks dead
rather than dim. Measured on the board 2026-09-16: after a menu had
been left on screen for 112 s, every tap was consumed as a wake event,
so not one button press reached the app underneath. The same dim made
A2DP playback look like the screen going black several times.

This default covers the whole UI -- Bluetooth audio, settings, the
launcher -- and survives a userdata wipe, which a runtime
`settings put` does not.

It is a third value in the overlay that is already here.
SettingsProvider keeps it in Settings.System rather than
Settings.Global, so the overlay's comment now names both, says three
values rather than two and gives the `settings put system` command for
an existing /data. The product makefile's DEVICE_PACKAGE_OVERLAYS line
moves below the hardware-decode block, next to the RRO that starts
Bluetooth off, and its comment names all three values and both tables.

The audio policy fork's third change raises bus0_media_out's gain
ceiling from trout's 600 to 1200 (+12 dB). MEDIA routes to that bus and
nothing else on the HDMI path adds gain: vc4-hdmi exposes no volume
control and the qemu ALSA backend has none. trout's audio HAL scales
the stream by 10^(gain_mB/2000), its own hard-coded range cancelling
out, and the formula keeps going above 600, so the top of the range
goes from about 2.0 (+6 dB) to about 4.0 (+12 dB). Measured on the
board, the HAL scaled the stream by 1.58 (400 mB) and HDMI audio was
too quiet. That is below what 600 allows, so the framework was not
sending the ceiling at the time; the volume index then is not
established, and the ratio with 1200 in place is not claimed. The
product makefile's note on the fork now says it documents all three
changes.

The README's Contents table listed five files and this device has
twenty-nine. Rewrite it, and add a section saying what each feature is
for -- Vulkan compositing, the Wi-Fi shadow AP, Bluetooth A2DP and
HFP, hardware decode -- so a reader can tell which files belong together
without reading every one of them. The paragraph under the old table,
about the build script that staged the files into the AOSP checkout,
is dropped.

The Vulkan note is longer than the others on purpose. Without all six
of its settings the guest still composites with GL, and the failure
mode when one is missing is not an error -- the numbers move by less
than run-to-run noise. Anyone reading this repository to find out
whether Vulkan is on needs the whole set named in one place, including
why the APEX has to go.

The note on what is expected to leave again says which part of the
overlay leaves with the rc: the two animation values. The dialer name
and the screen-off timeout stay, and so do the DomA feature files.

The features section names the workspace repository
(automotive-grade-linux/sodev-demo-workspace-rpi) and what each feature
relies on outside this repository: the workspace's patches to
packages/modules/Bluetooth, frameworks/base, packages/services/Car and
external/v4l2_codec2, the guest kernel's mac80211_hwsim and
virtio-media, and DomD.

Status keeps the record of the build that verified the move, scoped to
the files as they were then, and says what has and has not been done
since: the feature files were measured on hardware in the workspace
layer they came from, the boards differ in how far each feature was
taken, and no build of this revision is claimed.

README.md carries an SPDX identifier, as every file but LICENSE does.

